  [1mDesign[0m

  [1mApproach.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps its [38;5;153m(username, password)[39m signature. The stub gains a [38;5;153muserId[39m in its return value — standing in
  for what the real API at [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m would send back — and logs it once authentication "succeeds", so the log line
  records who logged in rather than who claimed to.

  [1mChanges, all in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m:[0m

  - [38;5;153mlogin()[39m (line 4-8): the stub return becomes [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username, userId }[39m, where [38;5;153muserId[39m is a placeholder
    derived in the stub. I'll mark it with a comment that the real implementation reads this from the API response,
    matching the existing [38;5;153m// Stub:[39m comment style at line 6.
  - [38;5;153mlogin()[39m: the existing [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username)[39m at line 5 stays as the pre-auth line; a second log after
    the stub result records the [38;5;153muserId[39m.
  - The submit handler (line 23) is unchanged — it already logs the whole result object at line 24, so the [38;5;153muserId[39m
    surfaces there automatically.

  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is untouched: no new form field, since nobody types a [38;5;153muserId[39m.

  [1mWhat this does not do.[0m It doesn't add real authentication, and the [38;5;153muserId[39m is fabricated by the stub — it becomes real
  only when [38;5;153mlogin()[39m actually POSTs to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. No [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter is added, per your choice; if you later want the
  caller to pass one, that's a separate decision with the spoofing tradeoff I flagged.

  [1mTesting.[0m No test suite exists to add to. I'll verify by reading the flow and, if you want, you can open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and
  check the console output on submit. I'm not going to add a test framework as part of this change unless you ask.

